  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nWinds will becoming north by sunrise in the wake of a cold front\nmoving across the area early this morning. Cooler and much less\nhumid air will filter into the region in the wake of this front.\nVisible satellite imagery from Monday afternoon showed smoke\nupstream, which the HRRR smoke model brings into our area \ntoday. Have adjusted sky cover up some to account for this once \nthe lower clouds this morning clear out. HRRR smoke model keeps \nnear surface smoke concentrations fairly low, and there aren't \nany meaningful visibility restrictions upstream, so not \nexpecting a repeat of the dense smoke from a couple of weeks \nago.\n\nGusty northerly winds down the full fetch of the lake will\nresult in large waves and dangerous currents through tonight\nand likely into Wednesday morning. A beach hazard statement\nremains in effect for this time period. \n\nDry and seasonable conditions are expected until the next system\narrives late in the week. GFS and ECMWF both amplify a shortwave\nas it approaches the Midwest Friday. GEFS and EPS both support\nthis system evolving into a closed mid-upper level low in the\nmid-Mississippi Valley Friday night into Saturday. Once the\nsystem closes off, guidance slows it down and its progged to\nmeander around the region this weekend. While there is good\nagreement between the GEFS and EPS means, it is worth noting\nthat medium range guidance often struggles with handling closing\noff systems like this, particularly in the summer when synoptic\npattern can frequently be significantly altered by convective \nprocesses. Having said all of that, the NBM pops for this period\nlook quite reasonable, with low end likely pops Friday night \ninto Saturday. While nothing off the charts, PWATs look to be \nseasonably high, so given the potential for a slow moving cut \noff low, there could be a heavy rainfall threat developing \nsomewhere in the Midwest this weekend. \n\nNBM is dry Sunday into early next week, but depending on where\nthat mid-upper level low meanders, it wouldn't be hard to\nenvision a scenario where rain chances need to be added to the\nforecast for Sunday and even into Monday. \n\n- Izzi",
